-- allium: 3
-- special-pages.allium

-- Scope: The pages by which Vera makes its governance visible — including
--   rector pages, administrative surfaces, derived registers and documentation
-- Includes: What makes a page special, what a special page may and may not
--   decide, who may change one, and what happens when what it says cannot be
--   understood
-- Excludes:
--   - The meaning of each particular setting (see workspace-interface.allium,
--     controlled-ontology.allium)
--   - How an agent is credentialed (see agent-participation.allium)
--   - Editing and rendering, which are the same as for any other page
-- Dependencies: Page, Block and Participant are governed by core.allium; the
--   authorship of what an agent writes by agent-participation.allium

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Vera described inside Vera.
--
-- What the corpus truly governs — such as its vocabulary and service
-- declarations — lives in pages written in the same Markdown as everything
-- else. Other special pages may instead administer state held elsewhere,
-- project derived records or document behaviour not yet wired to the program.
-- Their status is visible: being special never silently claims that prose is
-- executable configuration.
--
-- The reason is not elegance. A configuration file has no history, no author and
-- no provenance: it says what it says now, and nothing about who decided it or
-- when, or what it said before. A page has all of that already, because Vera
-- gives it to every page. Changing how the librarian works becomes a dated,
-- attributed, reversible change that can be read, argued with and undone — which
-- is what one wants from a decision, and never what a config file offers.
--
-- The price is circularity, and it is paid explicitly below rather than
-- discovered later.
contract SelfDescription {
    read_setting: (page: core/Page, key: String) -> String?
    propose_change: (participant: core/Participant, page: core/Page, content: String) -> Proposal

    @invariant DefaultsLiveInTheCode
        -- Every setting a special page can express has a value that holds when
        -- the page is absent, empty or unreadable. A special page overrides; it
        -- never supplies the floor.
        --
        -- Otherwise Vera would have to read a page to know how to draw the page,
        -- and a corpus whose ontology page was deleted would have no ontology
        -- rather than the starting one.

    @invariant ANewMemoryStartsWithVisibleGovernance
        -- Una instalación nueva no nace como un corpus desnudo que primero
        -- exige reconstruir las palabras con que se gobierna. Trae páginas
        -- rectoras legibles para propiedades, objetos y relaciones, además de
        -- la puerta de conexiones y el índice que las reúne.
        --
        -- Es un piso universal y editable, no una copia del corpus de quien
        -- distribuye Vera: las decisiones situadas se añaden después y cada
        -- declaración inicial conserva la procedencia de la distribución.

    @invariant ASpecialPageCannotBreakVera
        -- What a special page says is validated when read, never when written.
        -- A value that cannot be understood is ignored and reported, and the
        -- default stands. Nobody is ever prevented from editing a page of their
        -- own corpus, and no edit can leave Vera unable to open.

    @invariant AnAgentProposesToASpecialPage
        -- An agent may write anywhere in the corpus except a special page, where
        -- its changes arrive as proposals for a human to confirm.
        --
        -- This is the one place where the rule matters most: the librarian's
        -- standing instructions live in a corpus the librarian can write to, and
        -- an agent able to rewrite its own instructions is an agent under no
        -- instructions at all. Stated as a rule and not left to trust, because
        -- trust is exactly what an instruction is meant to establish.
        --
        -- It is a restriction on applying, not on participating: an agent that
        -- notices its instructions are wrong should say so, and this is how.

    @invariant SpecialityIsDeclaredNotGuessed
        -- A page is special because it says it is, in a property anyone can
        -- read and change. Vera does not keep a private list of page titles it
        -- treats differently: a rule that cannot be read from the corpus is a
        -- rule the corpus cannot be audited against.

    @invariant SpecialPagesShareAVisibleNamespace
        -- Every special page is titled `VERA: …`, so configuration pages gather
        -- visibly in search, links and page lists. The prefix is a convention
        -- for people, never how Vera decides that a page is special: only the
        -- declared special kind carries that authority.
        --
        -- Pages whose subject is Vera but which do not govern it remain ordinary
        -- prose and may use `VERA — …`; punctuation therefore distinguishes
        -- system governance from writing about the system without creating a
        -- second hidden classification rule.

    @invariant SpecialPagesArePermanentGovernance
        -- A page declared as `tipo=página especial` cannot be deleted. Its
        -- blocks remain ordinarily editable and every revision remains in the
        -- log, but the governing page itself is a permanent address of the
        -- instance rather than disposable corpus content.

    @invariant SpecialPagesAreNeitherTrailsNorProcessingMaterial
        -- A special page cannot be read as a trail and cannot be sent through
        -- automatic page processing. Its order describes governance rather
        -- than an argument, and its form and meaning are changed deliberately
        -- by its owner rather than inferred by a model.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a special page governs. Open on purpose: the list grows, and each new
-- kind is a decision to be argued rather than a value to be added quietly.
--
-- `service` and `connections` look alike and are opposites, and the difference decides
-- how many pages each gets. A service is an outgoing connection — Vera goes out,
-- with a key, a library, a set of collections — and no two outgoing services
-- share anything but the word: one page each. Connections are the other direction, one
-- door and many who come through it, distinguished only by who they are, what
-- they may do and what they took: one page, one row per connection. Four pages
-- would be four copies of the same three fields.
enum SpecialKind {
    ontology | properties | objects | relations | embeddings | presentation |
    instructions | publication | service | connections | activity | governance
}

enum SpecialMode { rector | surface | derived | documentation }

enum ProposalStatus { proposed | accepted | rejected }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A page that Vera reads for its own governance.
--
-- Not a different kind of page: a page carrying a property that says what it
-- governs. It has blocks, revisions, links and history like any other, and is
-- read and written in the same editor.
entity SpecialPage {
    graph: core/Graph
    page: core/Page
    kind: SpecialKind
    mode: SpecialMode
}

-- A change an agent wants made to a special page, waiting for a person.
entity Proposal {
    target: SpecialPage
    -- What the agent would have written, whole, so that accepting it is applying
    -- a text and not reconstructing an intention.
    content: String
    rationale: String
    status: ProposalStatus
    proposed_by: core/Participant
    proposed_at: Timestamp
    decided_by: core/Participant?
    decided_at: Timestamp?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A person writes to a special page like to any other page. There is no
-- ceremony: it is their corpus and their governance.
rule ParticipantEditsASpecialPage {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    let special = SpecialPage{page: change.page}

    requires: exists special
    requires: participant.kind = human

    ensures: change_applies
}

-- An agent's change to a special page becomes a proposal instead of an edit.
rule AgentProposesToASpecialPage {
    when: core/ParticipantSubmits(participant, graph, origin_id, change, channel, evidence?)

    let special = SpecialPage{page: change.page}

    requires: exists special
    requires: participant.kind = agent

    ensures: Proposal.created(
        target: special,
        content: change.content,
        rationale: "",
        status: proposed,
        proposed_by: participant,
        proposed_at: now,
        decided_by: null,
        decided_at: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- The submission is not refused and not silently dropped. It is
        -- redirected, and the agent is told so, because an agent that believes
        -- it wrote something it did not will go on reasoning from a corpus that
        -- does not exist.
}

-- Aceptar es aplicar el texto que el agente escribió, entero. No se reconstruye
-- una intención a partir de un resumen: lo que se acepta es lo que se leyó.
rule ParticipantAcceptsAProposal {
    when: AcceptProposal(participant, proposal)

    requires: participant.kind = human
    requires: proposal.status = proposed

    ensures:
        proposal.status = accepted
        proposal.decided_by = participant
        proposal.decided_at = now
        proposal.target.page.content = proposal.content
}

rule ParticipantRejectsAProposal {
    when: RejectProposal(participant, proposal)

    requires: participant.kind = human
    requires: proposal.status = proposed

    ensures:
        proposal.status = rejected
        proposal.decided_by = participant
        proposal.decided_at = now
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant OneKindPerSpecialPage {
    for special in SpecialPages:
        for other in SpecialPages:
            other.page = special.page implies other = special
}

invariant EachSpecialKindHasAnHonestMode {
    for special in SpecialPages:
        (special.kind = properties implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = objects implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = relations implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = embeddings implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = service implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = connections implies special.mode = rector) and
        (special.kind = publication implies special.mode = surface) and
        (special.kind = activity implies special.mode = derived) and
        (special.kind = governance implies special.mode = derived) and
        (special.kind = ontology implies special.mode = documentation) and
        (special.kind = presentation implies special.mode = documentation) and
        (special.kind = instructions implies special.mode = documentation)
}

-- El índice no se mantiene dos veces. La página es una dirección estable dentro
-- del grafo; sus renglones se derivan de todas las declaraciones `special-kind`
-- vigentes, incluida cualquier clase que este cliente todavía no conozca.
invariant GovernanceIndexesEverySpecialPage {
    for governance_page in SpecialPages where governance_page.kind = governance:
        governance_page.page.title = "VERA: Gobierno" and
        SpecialPages.all(special => governance_index(governance_page).contains(special))
}

-- La publicación tiene una sola puerta de gobierno por grafo. Su título no es
-- una clave oculta —el kind sigue siendo la autoridad—, pero sí la dirección
-- humana estable desde la que el dueño reconoce y enlaza esa configuración.
invariant OneCanonicalPublicationPagePerGraph {
    for special in SpecialPages:
        special.kind = publication implies
            special.page.title = "VERA: Publicación" and
            SpecialPages.all(other =>
                other.kind != publication or
                other.graph != special.graph or
                other = special
            )
}

invariant DecidedProposalsNameTheirDecider {
    for proposal in Proposals:
        proposal.status != proposed implies proposal.decided_by != null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface SpecialPageAdministration {
    facing owner: core/Participant
    context special: SpecialPage

    exposes:
        special.kind
        special.page

    provides:
        AcceptProposal(owner, proposal)
        RejectProposal(owner, proposal)

    contracts:
        fulfils SelfDescription

    @guarantee ASpecialPageSaysWhatItGoverns
        -- Reading a special page tells a participant whether its declarations
        -- rule behaviour, administer state held elsewhere, project derived
        -- records or merely document an intended behaviour. A page that looks
        -- executable while nobody reads it is worse than ordinary prose.

    @guarantee WhatVeraCouldNotUnderstandIsSaid
        -- When a special page contains something Vera cannot read, it says so
        -- on that page, naming the line. Falling back to a default in silence
        -- leaves a participant editing a setting that has never taken effect.

    @guarantee EverythingAConfigurationNamesIsFollowable
        -- What a special page lists — a type, a property, a concept, a theme
        -- token or instruction — is reached from
        -- where it is listed. These are not strings in a settings screen: each
        -- is a page of the corpus with an author, a history and a definition,
        -- and the configuration is a view onto them rather than the place they
        -- live.
        --
        -- This is the SelfDescription contract taken to its end. Having argued
        -- that what governs Vera lives in the corpus as pages, a settings screen
        -- that then printed those pages as inert text would put the argument
        -- exactly one click out of reach — and it is the click that makes the
        -- difference between a configuration one can read about and one one can
        -- only edit.

    @guarantee ProposalsAreVisibleWhereTheyApply
        -- An agent's pending proposals are seen on the page they would change,
        -- not in a queue elsewhere. A proposal nobody encounters is a proposal
        -- nobody decides, and the agent waits for ever.

    @guarantee DestructiveAndInterpretiveActionsAreAbsent
        -- The page-level actions do not offer deleting, reading as a trail or
        -- automatic processing for a special page. The same restrictions hold
        -- at the writing boundary, so another client cannot bypass the page.

    @guarantee SpecialityDoesNotPretendToBeExecution
        -- A special page whose mode is documentation never says that editing
        -- its blocks changes running behaviour. A surface or derived page says
        -- where its effective state comes from instead of presenting its prose
        -- as a second source of truth.
}

surface GovernanceIndex {
    facing participant: core/Participant
    context governance_page: SpecialPage where governance_page.kind = governance

    exposes:
        governance_page.page
        governance_index(governance_page)

    @guarantee TheIndexIsDerivedAndFollowable
        -- Cada renglón enlaza a la página especial real y dice su clase, modo y
        -- efecto. No copia sus bloques ni guarda una segunda lista que pueda
        -- quedarse atrás. Una declaración desconocida se muestra como tal en vez
        -- de desaparecer.

    @guarantee ConfigurationAndThePageReadTheSameDeclarations
        -- Configuración y `VERA: Gobierno` son dos superficies de los mismos
        -- `special-kind`; ninguna mantiene un inventario privado distinto.

    @guarantee AFirstVisitHasSomethingToGovern
        -- Antes de que exista contenido propio, el índice ya permite alcanzar
        -- las páginas rectoras del vocabulario y la puerta de conexiones. Una
        -- lista vacía en una instalación nueva es un defecto de arranque, no
        -- una invitación a conocer de antemano la ontología interna de Vera.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Can a participant make an ordinary page special after the fact, and what happens to the history of what it said while it was ordinary?"
open question "When two special pages of the same kind exist — by import, by copy, by mistake — which governs, and is the answer a rule or a refusal?"
open question "Does a special page belong in the public projection of a graph, given that it describes how the instance is run rather than what its owner thinks?"
open question "Should a rejected proposal remain readable on the page it targeted, as a record of what an agent wanted and was refused, or does that turn every page into an argument?"
